;;; DRAFT -- reverse!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/reverse-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "reverse!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(reverse! lst) -> list"
  :summary "The elements of lst in reverse order, built by turning lst's own cells around. The argument is destroyed."
  :explanation "reverse allocates one cell per element and leaves its argument alone. reverse! allocates nothing: it walks the spine turning every cdr to point backwards, so the cell that was first becomes last and the cell that was last is returned. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. The specific shape of the damage is worth stating because it is not the usual one -- after (reverse! xs), xs still names a valid list, but it is the ONE-ELEMENT list holding what used to be the first element. It has not become empty and it has not become the reversed list. That is the most confusing outcome in this whole family, because the variable still works and still prints, and it is wrong. There is no test for reverse! anywhere in the conformance suite (list.sld names it at :84 with no body), so this record rests on the structure of the operation rather than on evidence from the repo."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(display (reverse! (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; xs is left naming the one-element list (1), not the reversed list.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define r (reverse! xs))\n(display (list 'reversed r 'xs-now xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; the pure form is what you want unless the argument is private.\n(define shared (list 1 2 3))\n(define safe (reverse shared))\n(display (list 'safe safe 'shared-intact shared))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed. After the call the argument variable names a one-element list holding what was the first element -- it does not name the reversed list and it is not empty."
             "That outcome is a valid list, so nothing signals the error. This is the most quietly wrong of the linear-update verbs."
             "There is NO test for reverse! in scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld (named at :84, no body). This record is derived from the structure of an in-place reversal, not from in-repo evidence."
             "reverse! is append-reverse! with an empty tail; if you need to attach something afterwards, use append-reverse! and save a pass.")
  :drawbacks ("The half-valid leftover binding is a worse failure mode than an outright error would be."
             "It saves allocation only, and reverse is already a single linear pass."
             "Nothing in the engine or the reader can warn about a stale reference to a reversed list.")
  :usecases ("Flipping a private accumulator at the end of a loop when nothing else holds it."
             "In-place reversal of a large freshly-built list where the allocation is measured and material."
             "Implementing other list verbs internally, where the ownership argument is local and checkable.")
  :related ("reverse"
             "append-reverse!"
             "append!"
             "list-copy"
             "fold")
  :learn (:concept "When the wreckage is still a valid value"
          :prerequisites ("reverse" "set-cdr!" "append-reverse!" "eq?")
          :progression "Reverse a list purely. Then reason about what the argument variable must point at after an in-place reversal, before you run it. That prediction is the lesson.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; NAMED at scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:84 with NO TEST BODY -- no in-repo behavioural evidence exists for this verb. Signature derived from the reverse record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat; semantics derived from the structure of an in-place spine reversal. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(display (reverse! (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(display (equal? (reverse a) (reverse! b)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define r (reverse! xs))\n(display (list 'reversed r 'argument-variable-now xs))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a backwards accumulator and wants it forwards. Candidates: reverse (one pass, n cells allocated, argument intact), reverse! (one pass, nothing allocated, argument left naming a one-element list), or append-reverse! if anything is to be attached afterwards (one pass, nothing allocated, and no separate append). If a tail exists at all, the third is strictly better than the first two."
     :explanation "eng: the reversed list comes back and the old variable is left holding a stub. math: reverse! agrees with reverse as a value on every proper list. cs: the stub is what makes this verb dangerous -- a destroyed binding that still type-checks and still prints."
     :meta "Ask what the argument variable points at afterwards. For most of this family the answer is \"a truncated list\"; for reverse! it is \"a one-element list\", and that surprises people.")
    (:eng "Predict the wreckage before you run the verb. If you cannot, do not use the destructive form."
     :math "Value agreement with reverse is total; the argument variable is where they part."
     :cs "A destroyed binding that remains a valid value is the failure mode static checking cannot reach.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Turning a sequence around by re-pointing its links, rather than by building a new one."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (reverse! (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a train run round a loop" :code "(define rake (list 'loco 'car1 'van))\n(display (reverse! rake))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a stack emptied into another stack" :code "(define s (list 'top 'mid 'bottom))\n(display (reverse! s))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, every cdr is turned to point at its predecessor. A train run round a loop comes back with the van leading and the loco at the rear; no wagons were built and the rake as it was does not exist any more. A stack emptied into another stack arrives in the opposite order for the same reason. All three share an order change achieved by moving connections rather than contents."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever reversal is a property of traversal rather than of storage.")
    (:eng "Moving the connections is cheaper than moving the contents, and it destroys the original arrangement."
     :math "Reversal is an involution: doing it twice returns the original order."
     :cs "Pointer reversal is the classic in-place list algorithm and the one every linked-list interview asks for.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define r (reverse! (list 1 2 3)))\n(display (list 'r r 'len (length r)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (involution? xs)\n  (equal? (reverse (reverse xs)) xs))\n(display (list (involution? (list)) (involution? (list 1)) (involution? (list 1 2 3))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define r (reverse! xs))\n(display (list 'argument-is-now-a-stub (equal? xs (list 1)) 'result-is-old-last-cell (equal? r (list 3 2 1))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a proper list lst, reverse! returns a list equal? to (reverse lst). The returned list is built from lst's own cells, and the argument variable is left naming a one-element list holding the original first element. reverse! is append-reverse! with an empty tail."
     :invariant "forall proper lst. equal?(reverse!(lst), reverse(lst)) and reverse!(lst) = append-reverse!(lst, ())"
     :meta "The pattern is in-place pointer reversal. What would falsify the claim: disagreement with reverse, or an argument variable left naming something other than a one-element list -- but note that nothing in the repo tests this, so the second half of the claim is derivation, not evidence, and it is why the record is marked needs-alfred.")
    (:eng "Say plainly which half of a claim is evidence and which is derivation. The reader cannot tell otherwise."
     :math "Involution is the property worth testing on the pure form; the destructive form cannot be applied twice to check it."
     :cs "The absence of a conformance test is itself a finding, and it belongs in the record rather than in a side note.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (flip! xs) (reverse! xs))\n(display (flip! (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (reverse-via-append-reverse xs) (append-reverse xs (list)))\n(display (reverse-via-append-reverse (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (palindrome? xs) (equal? xs (reverse xs)))\n(display (list (palindrome? (list 1 2 1)) (palindrome? (list 1 2))))\n(newline)"
     :base "reverse! turns a list around by re-pointing its cells."
     :emergent "eng: the flip step at the end of an accumulator loop. math: reverse expressed through append-reverse shows the two are one verb, and it is the form that generalises. cs: a palindrome check needs the argument twice, so it can never use the destructive form -- the second reference is already gone."
     :meta "The family is order inversion. What emerges is a simple test for whether a destructive verb is admissible at all: count the references to the argument after the call. One is fine, two is a bug.")
    (:eng "Count the uses of the argument after the call. More than none means the pure verb."
     :math "A predicate comparing a value to its own reversal needs both, which rules out in-place reversal by construction."
     :cs "The reference count is the cheapest static check a reviewer can apply to a bang verb.")
  )
)
